          "text": "## Review: Crucible — Adversarial Multi-Agent Debate Framework\n\nI've read the entire codebase. Here's my honest assessment.\n\n---\n\n### What It Gets Right\n\n**1. The core insight is sound.** Single-agent responses have a well-known failure mode: the model commits to its first approach and rationalizes it. Adversarial debate forces genuine stress-testing of ideas. This is one of the best-validated approaches in multi-agent AI literature.\n\n**2. Self-critique before cross-attack (Round 1.5) is clever.** Agents harden their positions before opponents see them. This avoids the pattern where an agent's first draft is trivially destroyed, wasting a round. The debate starts at a higher floor.\n\n**3. Adaptive focus areas prevent rehashing.** The convergence check extracts specific disagreements (`FOCUS:` lines) and funnels the next round into those unresolved points. Without this, agents tend to repeat the same attacks round after round.\n\n**4. Progressive summarization is well-thought-out.** Full transcripts for N agents over K rounds would blow context limits. Digesting early rounds but keeping the final round in full detail is a pragmatic solution — the arbiter sees the arc without drowning in text.\n\n**5. The provenance system is a strong differentiator.** Tagging every element of the final answer with `[AGREED]`, `[CONTESTED → ruling]`, `[AGENT X]`, `[SHARED-BIAS OVERRIDE]`, or `[ARBITER ADDITION]` gives the user a transparency layer most multi-agent systems lack entirely. You can trace claims back to their source.\n\n**6. The arbiter audit is good defense-in-depth.** Having a separate Sonnet agent check the arbiter for unsupported additions catches the failure mode where the arbiter \"hallucinate-synthesizes\" beyond what the debate actually established.\n\n**7. Model diversity as default** — alternating Opus/Sonnet — is well-motivated. Different models have genuinely different blind spots and reasoning patterns.\n\n---\n\n### Where It's Weak or Risky\n\n**1. Cost and latency scale brutally.** The all-pairs cross-attack means N agents produce N×(N-1) critiques per round. With 5 agents and 5 rounds, that's 100 critique calls + 25 defense calls + 5 convergence checks + proposals + self-critiques + arbitration + audit. You're looking at ~150 agent calls. That's expensive and slow. The default of 2 agents is wise, but the system should be more aggressive about warning users of the cost curve.\n\n**2. Convergence detection is fragile.** A single Sonnet agent decides `CONVERGED` vs `DIVERGED` by reading all positions. This is a subjective judgment call. Problems:\n   - Two agents can use different words for the same approach → premature convergence\n   - Two agents can agree on surface structure but differ on critical details → false convergence\n   - The convergence checker has no scoring rubric — it's vibes-based\n   \n   A more robust approach would extract structured position summaries and compare them dimension-by-dimension.\n\n**3. Personas can become theater.** The personas (Correctness-First, Simplicity-First, Devil's Advocate, etc.) are a good idea in principle, but in practice LLMs often converge to the same answer regardless of persona framing — especially for tasks with clear correct answers. The persona may add flavor to the critique style without actually producing genuinely different solutions. For design/architecture tasks this works well; for \"implement X correctly\" tasks, it may just add cost.\n\n**4. No mechanism for agents to share evidence across rounds.** Agent Alpha might discover via WebSearch that a particular API is deprecated. Beta never learns this unless Alpha explicitly mentions it in their defense. There's no shared evidence pool. This means agents can talk past each other on factual questions for multiple rounds.\n\n**5. The shared-bias check is aspirational.** The arbiter instructions say \"when all agents agree, ask if they could all be wrong.\" This is the hardest thing 