ill use ~X agent calls. Proceed?\" Especially for N>2.\n\n2. **Structured convergence checking.** Instead of asking Sonnet \"did they converge?\", have agents emit a structured position vector (key decisions as key-value pairs) and compare those programmatically.\n\n3. **Shared evidence board.** After each round, extract `[EVIDENCE]` citations into a shared context block all agents can see. This prevents factual disagreements from persisting.\n\n4. **Early termination for clear winners.** If after Round 1.5 self-critique, one agent's solution is strictly superior and the other has conceded major points, skip to arbitration instead of forcing more rounds.\n\n5. **Tiered debate.** For simple tasks, skip the full adversarial loop — just do propose → self-critique → arbiter. Reserve the full cross-attack loop for tasks the complexity assessment rates as 3+ agents.\n\n---\n\n### Verdict\n\n**It's a well-designed system for its target use case.** The structured phases (propose → self-critique → cross-attack → defend → converge → arbitrate → audit) are logical and well-sequenced. The provenance tracking and audit layer set it apart from naive \"just run two agents\" approaches.\n\nThe main risk is that it's **expensive for the marginal correctness gain** on simpler tasks. The architecture assumes the problem is hard enough to justify 10-150 agent calls. When it is — complex design decisions, high-stakes trade-offs — this is genuinely valuable. When it isn't, a single agent with good prompting gets you 90% of the way at 1% of the cost.\n\nThe system would benefit most from better task-type routing (not everything needs a full debate) and a shared evidence mechanism (so agents don't talk past each other on facts).",
